<rsd>

  <title>The truth of Christianity</title>

  <line mode="h1" justify="center">
    The truth of Christianity
  </line>

  <line mode="h3" justify="center">
    <i>Why we need Jesus, and why some people in the USA start not believing in Him</i>
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line>
    <b>Christianity</b> has shaped cultures, inspired compassion, and offered hope to many people across history.
    At the center of this faith stands <highlight co="#FFF59D">Jesus</highlight> — whose teachings about
    <i>forgiveness</i>, <i>love</i>, and <i>redemption</i> continue to influence the world.
  </line>

  <line>
    Yet in the <color co="#1976D2">United States</color>, a country once deeply rooted in Christian identity,
    more people are beginning to drift away from belief. Understanding both sides —
    <b>why we need Jesus</b> and <b>why some stop believing</b> — helps us see the full picture of Christianity today.
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line mode="h2">1. Why many people believe we need Jesus</line>

  <line><b>A. A source of hope in a broken world</b></line>
  <line>
    Many Christians see Jesus as the answer to the deepest human struggles: guilt, fear, loneliness,
    and the longing for meaning. His message that every person is loved and redeemable gives people
    a foundation to stand on when life feels unstable.
  </line>

  <line><b>B. A moral compass</b></line>
  <line>
    Jesus’ teachings — <i>love your neighbor</i>, <i>forgive your enemies</i>, <i>care for the poor</i> —
    offer a moral framework that many find both challenging and life‑giving.
    For believers, following Jesus is not just religion; it is a way of living with purpose.
  </line>

  <line><b>C. A path to grace</b></line>
  <line>
    Christianity teaches that humans are imperfect, and that Jesus bridges the gap between human brokenness
    and God’s holiness. This idea of <highlight co="#C8E6C9">grace</highlight> — being accepted not because we are perfect,
    but because we are loved — is one of the most powerful reasons people turn to Him.
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line mode="h2">2. Why some in the USA are losing faith</line>

  <line><b>A. Cultural shifts</b></line>
  <line>
    American society has become more individualistic and less tied to traditional institutions.
    As people prioritize personal freedom and self‑definition, organized religion can feel restrictive
    or outdated to some.
  </line>

  <line><b>B. Negative experiences with churches</b></line>
  <line>
    Some people step away not because of Jesus, but because of painful experiences with religious communities —
    hypocrisy, judgment, or political division. These experiences can overshadow the core message of Christianity.
  </line>

  <line><b>C. The rise of science and secularism</b></line>
  <line>
    As scientific understanding grows, some feel they must choose between faith and reason.
    Even though many Christians see no conflict between the two, the cultural narrative often pushes them apart.
  </line>

  <line><b>D. Information overload</b></line>
  <line>
    The internet exposes people to countless worldviews, philosophies, and criticisms of religion.
    This can create confusion or skepticism, especially for those who never had strong spiritual foundations.
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line mode="h2">3. The tension: needing Jesus vs. drifting away</line>

  <line>
    The paradox is striking:
    <highlight co="#FFE082">Christianity continues to offer hope, identity, and moral grounding,
    yet many Americans feel disconnected from it.</highlight>
  </line>

  <line>
    Some see Jesus as the answer to modern anxiety, division, and emptiness.
    Others feel that religion no longer speaks to their experiences or values.
    This tension reflects a society searching for meaning in a rapidly changing world.
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line mode="h2">4. A path forward</line>

  <line>
    Whether someone believes or doubts, the conversation about Jesus remains deeply relevant.
    His teachings about compassion, humility, and sacrificial love continue to challenge and inspire.
  </line>

  <line>
    For those who still believe, the mission is not to win arguments but to live out the kind of love Jesus taught —
    because often, people lose faith not from lack of truth, but from lack of love.
  </line>

  <line></line>

  <line justify="center"><i>“The question is not only whether Jesus is true,”</i></line>
  <line justify="center"><i>“but whether we are willing to live the love He spoke of.”</i></line>

  <line></line>

  <line>
    For more reflections, you might visit
    <link url="https://example.com/christianity-truth">this article</link>
    or write your own story in this RSD document.
  </line>

  <line>
    Sometimes people feel their faith is <s>completely gone</s>, but often it is simply buried under pain or confusion.
  </line>

  <line>
    The message of Jesus is still <size s="50">BIG</size> enough to reach anyone who seeks truth.
  </line>

  <line>
    And His love remains <u>unchanging</u>, even when our beliefs shift.
  </line>

  <line>
    As one writer beautifully expressed it in a different style:
    <font name="Lexend">“Grace is not earned; it is received.”</font>
  </line>

  <line>Below is a short summary using a list:</line>

  <list order="true">
    <line>Jesus offers hope and identity.</line>
    <line>Modern culture creates spiritual distance.</line>
    <line>Faith grows through love, not pressure.</line>
  </list>

	<blockquote>love the lord your god with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.</blockquote>
	
	<line>
	God Bless you.
	</line>

